                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Mythlink is a free VR monster battling game where we choose a creature, train it through different activities and then take it into battles. The main idea is that we are linked to our Mythlink, so improving ourselves through the training games also improves our monster. It is a pretty unusual idea for a VR game, and I like that it tries to make the player part of the progression rather than just controlling a creature from a distance.\n\nThere are three Mythlinks to choose from, Minorod, Galest and Urusagi, and they all have different abilities and styles. Minorod focuses on lightning and earth, Galest uses wind and dark abilities, while Urusagi uses sound and light. Each one also has more than 25 abilities to work towards, so there is some room to build them differently rather than having three creatures that only look different.\n\nThe training games are probably the most interesting part for me. There are six different minigames covering strength, speed and brain, and each one is designed around VR interaction. We can do things like punching exercises and other physical challenges, then use the results to improve our Mythlink. It is a fun idea, although some of the minigames feel more like simple training exercises than fully developed games.\n\nThe actual battles are turn based and let us bring a custom selection of abilities into a fight. We can use up to nine abilities in a battle, which gives the combat a bit more strategy once we start understanding what each ability does. There is also online 1v1 battling, although the small size of the game means I would not expect a huge amount of activity around the multiplayer side.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is the limited amount of content. There are only three creatures and six training games, so I can get a pretty good idea of what Mythlink offers without needing a huge amount of time. The training is fun at first, but repeating the same activities to improve stats can start feeling more like grinding than playing.\n\nThe VR presentation is also quite basic. The low poly style fits the small indie nature of the game, and I like that the developer clearly designed the systems around room scale VR, but it does not have the polish of a bigger VR production. Some of the interactions can also feel a little awkward, especially when I am trying to get used to how everything works.\n\nI do like the fact that Mythlink is completely free now. It originally launched as a paid game in 2023 before being made free in January 2025, so there is a lot more value here when I can try the full game without paying anything. There are also 15 achievements, including some that require high scores and winning online matches.\n\nOverall, Mythlink is a strange but genuinely interesting VR experiment. I like the idea of personally training alongside a monster and then taking that creature into battle, and the different minigames give the progression something different from a normal RPG. The small roster, basic presentation and limited long term variety hold it back, but since the full game is free, I think it is worth trying if I already have compatible VR equipment.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
